### Example with Retry Mechanism

If you want to add a retry mechanism for transient failures, you can use a loop to retry the request a few times before failing:

```java
import io.restassured.RestAssured;
import io.restassured.response.Response;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

public class SensorSyncTest {
    private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(SensorSyncTest.class);
    private static final int MAX_RETRIES = 3;

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Set up REST-assured
        RestAssured.baseURI = "https://example.com";

        // Run sensor sync checks
        for (int i = 0; i < 500; i++) {
            boolean success = false;
            int retries = 0;

            while (!success && retries < MAX_RETRIES) {
                try {
                    Response response = RestAssured.given()
                            .when()
                            .get("/sensor-sync");

                    // Log the response status code
                    logger.info("Iteration {}: Status code = {}", i, response.getStatusCode());

                    // Assert the status code
                    response.then().statusCode(200);

                    // Optionally, log the response body
                    logger.debug("Iteration {}: Response body = {}", i, response.getBody().asString());

                    success = true;
                } catch (Exception e) {
                    // Log any exceptions that occur
                    logger.error("Iteration {} failed (retry {}): {}", i, retries + 1, e.getMessage(), e);
                    retries++;
                }
            }

            if (!success) {
                logger.error("Iteration {} failed after {} retries", i, MAX_RETRIES);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### Explanation

- **Retry Mechanism**: Added a retry loop to attempt the request up to `MAX_RETRIES` times before failing.
- **Logging**: Enhanced logging to track the number of retries.

By following these guidelines, you can improve the robustness and maintainability of your proof of concept test.

